# 🚀 Day 3 - Palindrome Number

## 📌 Problem
**LeetCode #9 - Palindrome Number**

Determine whether an integer is a palindrome without converting it to a string.

---

## 🏷️ Difficulty
Easy

---

## 📚 Topic
- Math
- Number Manipulation

---

## 💡 Approach

Instead of reversing the entire number, reverse only half of it.

- Negative numbers are never palindromes.
- If a number ends with 0 (except 0 itself), it cannot be a palindrome.
- Reverse digits until the reversed half becomes greater than or equal to the remaining half.
- Compare both halves.

---

## ⚡ Complexity

- **Time Complexity:** O(log₁₀ n)
- **Space Complexity:** O(1)

---

## 📝 Key Learnings

- Reversing only half the digits avoids integer overflow.
- Mathematical solutions can be more efficient than string-based solutions.
- Handle edge cases before implementing the main logic.

---

## ✅ Status

Accepted ✔️



## Code


#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    bool isPalindrome(int x) {
        if (x < 0 || (x % 10 == 0 && x != 0))
            return false;

        int reversedHalf = 0;

        while (x > reversedHalf) {
            reversedHalf = reversedHalf * 10 + x % 10;
            x /= 10;
        }

        return (x == reversedHalf || x == reversedHalf / 10);
    }
};

int main() {
    Solution obj;

    int x = 121;

    if (obj.isPalindrome(x))
        cout << "true";
    else
        cout << "false";

    return 0;
}
